 id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Selection of subepithelial lesions.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-24-101-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Bite-on-bite biopsy with conventional-sized forceps. (A) Incidental subepithelial lesion in the esophagus during diagnostic endoscopy, (B), (C), and (D) each bite is directly on top of the previous bite in an attempt to burrow into the lesion.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-24-101-g002"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Diagnostic yield for the esophagus, stomach, and duodenum</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-24-101-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p><sup>*</sup>Gastrointestinal stromal tumor</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Diagnostic yield for the esophagus versus stomach and duodenum</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-24-101-i002"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p><italic>p</italic>=0.109</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Relationship between diagnostic yield and size of the lesion</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjim-24-101-i003"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p><italic>p</italic>=0.495</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>